{"name":"Almandine","hex":"#c2a37e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"almandine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of garnet having a deep red color, inclining to purple, with the chemical formula Fe3Al2(SiO4)3."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/almandine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Almandine","blocks":[{"text":"Almandine, also known as almandite, is a mineral belonging to the garnet group. The name is a corruption of alabandicus, which is the name applied by Pliny the Elder to a stone found or worked at Alabanda, a town in Caria in Asia Minor. Almandine is an iron aluminium garnet, of deep red color, inclining to purple. It is frequently cut with a convex face, or en cabochon, and is then known as carbuncle. Viewed through the spectroscope in a strong light, it generally shows three characteristic absorption bands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Almandin.jpg/330px-Almandin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almandine","wikidata":"Q273663"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg/500px-Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg/1280px-Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg/500px-Victorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 19th-century almandine garnet brooch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVictorian_almandine_garnet_brooch.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"1995 UAZ 2206 beige front","image":"https://t